elasiticity of demand:
    the elasticity of demand is a units-free measure of the responsiveness of the quantitiy demanded of a good to a change in its price when all other influences on buying plans remain the same.

How to measure:
    percentage change in the quantity demanded / percentage change in the price

    percentage change in the quantity = (Q new - Q old)/((Q new + Q old)/2)

    percentage change in the price = (P new - P old)/((P new + P old)/2)

    or simply -> (ΔQ/Q average)/(ΔP/P average)

the status of elasticity of demand:
    1. perfectly inelastic demand:
        if the quantity demanded doesn't change when the price changes. the price elasticity of demand is zero and the good has a perfectly inelastic demand

    2. unit elastic demand:
        if the percentage change in the quantity demanded equals the percentage change in price, the price elasticity of demand equals 1 and the good has unit elastic demand

    3. inelastic demand:
        if the percentage change in the quantity demanded is smaller than the percentage change in price, the price elasticity of demand is less than 1 (abs value)and the good has inelastic demand

    4. elastic demand:
        if the percntage change in the quantity demanded is greater than the percentage change in price, the price elasticity of demand is greater than 1 (abs value) the good has elastic demand.

    5. perfectly elastic demand:
        if the percentage change in the quantity demanded is infinitely large when the price barely changes, the price elasticity of demand is infinite and the good has a perfectly elastic demand

the factors of elasticity of demand:
    the closeness of substitutes:
        the closer the substitutes for a good or service, the more elastic is the demand for the good or service

    the proportion of income spent on the good:
        the greater proportion of income consumers spend on good, the larger is the elasticity of demand for that good


    the time elapsed since a price change；
        the more time consumers have to adjust to a price change, or the longer that a good can be stored without losing its value,the more elastic is the demand for that good

the elasticity of linear demand curve:
    the midpoint of linear demand curve is the unit elasticity

    the half graph above the midpoint is elastic

    the half graph lower the midpoint is inelastic

Total revenue and elasticity:
    the total revenue from the sale of a good or service equals the price of the good multiplied by the quantity sold

    when the price changes, total revenue also changes：
        the change in total revenue due to a change in price depends on the elasticity of demand:
            1. if demand is elastic, a 1 percent price cut increases the quantity sold by more than 1 percent, and total revenue increases

            2. if demand is inelastic, a 1 percent price cut increases the quantity sold by less than 1, and total revenues decreases

            3. if demand is unit elastic, a 1 percent price cut increases the quantity sold by q percent, and total revenue remains unchanged

    the total revenue test:
        it is a method of estimating the price elasticity of demand by observing the change in total revenue that results from a price

            - if a price cut increases total revenue, demand is elastic
            - if a price cut decreases total revenue, demand is inelastic
            - if a price cut leaves total revenue unchanged, demand is unit elastic


    the expenditure and elasticity；
        
        - If your demand is elastic, a 1 percent price cut increases the quantity you buy by more than 1 percent and your expenditure on the item increases (i.e. for me, comic books).

        - If your demand is inelastic, a 1 percent price cut increases the quantity you buy by less than 1 percent and your expenditure on the item decreases (i.e. for me, gasoline).

        - If your demand is unit elastic, a 1 percent price cut increases the quantity you buy by 1 percent and your expenditure on the item does not change (i.e. for me, most food items – like fruit).

income elasticity of demand：
    DEF: the income elasticity of demand is a units-free measure of the resposiveness of the quantity demanded of a good to a change in consumer income when all other influences on buying plans remain the same

    similar to elasticity of demand, the calculation is:
        percentage change in the quantity demanded / percentage change in income

    classification of goods:
        if the income elasticity of demand is greater than 1:
            - demand is income elastic
            - good is as normal good

        if the income elasticity of demand is greater than zero but less than 1
            - demand is come inelastic
            - good is as normal good
        
        if the income elasticity of demand is less than 0
            - the good is classified as an inferior good

cross elasticity of demand；
    DEF: the cross elasticity of demand is a units-free measure of the responsiveness of the quantity demanded of a good to a change in the price of a substitute or a complement when all other influences on buying plans remain the same

    the equation of calculating:
        percentage change in the quantity demanded of good X/ percentage change in the price of good Y

        note: X Y can be substitute or complement

        The cross elasticity of demand for a substitute is positive

        the cross elasticity of complement is negative